WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:05.318
Before you enable a redaction rule, test it
against a sample request from staging.

00:00:05.489 --> 00:00:09.113
Let's use a synthetic checkout payload with
an email address.

00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:12.582
This rule extracts the email from the JSON

00:00:12.582 --> 00:00:16.489
request body and passes it to the D L P
field transform.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.833
Keep a copy of the input and choose a

00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:21.711
record that also has fields the replay will
need.

00:00:22.252 --> 00:00:25.768
Run proxymock D L P test on the staged
recording.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:28.550
The result shows one recorded request would

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.344
be redacted and identifies the matching
field.

00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:33.582
This preview leaves the file as it was.

00:00:34.152 --> 00:00:36.879
Inspect the before and after for the same
request.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.033
The synthetic email became a redaction
token.

00:00:40.204 --> 00:00:44.826
The order identifier, currency, amount, and
response status still match.

00:00:45.385 --> 00:00:50.972
If you need a copy to inspect, run D L P
apply and write it to a separate directory.

00:00:51.143 --> 00:00:56.108
Then send another synthetic request through
a staging forwarder configured with the

00:00:56.108 --> 00:00:59.638
rule and confirm the captured result before
rolling it out.

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:05.255
Keep the before and after example so the
next person can verify what the rule does.
